Subject: Run opcache's JIT under Fil-C by compiling its IR as C
Origin: Filnix
Upstream-Status: Not submitted

Fil-C cannot run machine code written into memory, so opcache's DynASM
backend cannot work. This adds a second backend (ext/opcache/jit/zend_jit_c.c)
used when building with Fil-C: each IR function the JIT produces is printed as
C, compiled by the Fil-C compiler into a shared object and loaded with dlopen.
The compiler instruments the generated code like any other C, so a JIT bug
gives wrong results or a Fil-C panic, never memory corruption.

Fil-C pointers carry an invisible capability that integers lack, and the JIT
treats addresses as integers:

- IR address values are char * in the generated C; additions keep the
  capability of the pointer operand, chosen at run time when both may be one.
- Every pointer the JIT embeds as a constant is registered when it becomes an
  integer (ir_CONST_ADDR, ZEND_ADDR_CONST_ZVAL); generated code reads it back
  from a table the loader fills, and Z_ZV looks it up again.
- The VM's fixed registers (execute_data, opline) become C locals; code that
  the IR reaches by jumping receives them as arguments.

Function JIT works as designed. In the tracing JIT a failed guard copies its
snapshot's values into an emulated register buffer and calls a C version of
the CALL VM's exit stub around zend_jit_trace_exit(). Generated code cannot be
patched, so with the C backend side traces are compiled but never linked,
and exits fall back to the interpreter; traces also compile synchronously,
which makes tracing slower than the interpreter on short scripts.

The JIT buffer stays ordinary memory (no mprotect), and opcache no longer
picks a mapping hint near PHP's text.

PHP 8.5's CALL VM handlers take (execute_data, opline) and return the next
opline tagged with ZEND_VM_ENTER_BIT; the C trace-exit stub follows that.

Compiled objects can be cached across processes. The generated C names every
pointer through the zjc_K table, so its text determines the code; the cache
key hashes it with zend_system_id, the compiler and its flags. To keep the
text independent of the process, constants that constant folding derived
from registered pointers are expressed relative to pointers the same IR
registered, the opline comparison uses whole pointers, and jump tables'
bucket addresses are loaded instead of embedded. A hit still builds the IR
and prints the C, but skips the compiler.

Environment: PHP_JIT_CC (compiler; the default ZEND_JIT_C_CC is meant to be
replaced by an absolute path), PHP_JIT_CFLAGS (-O1), PHP_JIT_C_DIR (/tmp),
PHP_JIT_C_CACHE (colon-separated cache directories; new objects go to the
first), PHP_JIT_C_CACHE_ONLY (leave misses to the interpreter),
PHP_JIT_C_MAX_SIZE (bytes of C above which a function stays interpreted,
default 512 KiB; such functions take minutes to compile),
PHP_JIT_C_STATS (print counts and times at exit), PHP_JIT_C_LOG (append one
line per function), PHP_JIT_C_KEEP, PHP_JIT_C_VERBOSE. They are read once,
so FPM workers use the master's even with clear_env.

Both backends stop PHP's execution timer (ITIMER_PROF) while compiling, so
compile time does not count against max_execution_time: a request that
makes much code hot at once would otherwise fail where the interpreter
serves it.

Where the toolchain has Fil-C's in-process LLVM JIT (filc_llvm_jit.h), a
second backend (ext/opcache/jit/zend_jit_llvm.c) is the default: it builds
LLVM IR for each IR function and compiles it with FilLLVMJIT_CompileModule,
which always runs KillUB and FilPizlonator. It shares the C backend's pointer
typing, registry, fixed registers and trace exits; a registered pointer
becomes an external global bound to it with FilLLVMJIT_SetGlobalMapping.
Every IR value lives in a stack slot that SROA promotes, which also covers
ENTRY blocks. PHP must be linked with -enable-filc-jit.

The LLVM backend links side traces: each trace's exits go through a table
that zend_jit_link_side_trace fills, and a linked exit tail-calls the side
trace, which reads its parent's snapshot registers (RLOAD) from the exit
buffer. Unlinked exits deoptimize through the C exit stub. Exits that land
in a frame without a trace extension (code opcache did not cache, or a
__call trampoline, whose shared op_array carries other data in its reserved
slots) resume at that opline's own handler.

Function JIT adds each RECV/OSR entry as an ENTRY block whose fake input is
the preceding END; both backends, like IR's own emitters, never let that END
jump into the ENTRY, which resets FP and IP to the function's parameters and
broke direct recursive calls that IR turns into loops.

PHP_JIT_BACKEND=c|llvm selects the backend, PHP_JIT_LLVM_OPT its opt-level
(default 2), PHP_JIT_LLVM_OPTIONS=key=value,... further FilLLVMJIT_SetOption
options, PHP_JIT_LLVM_MAX_INSNS the largest IR function compiled (default
10000; compile time grows faster than size), PHP_JIT_LLVM_NOLINK=1 turns
side-trace linking off, PHP_JIT_LLVM_DUMP=1 prints each module with its IR
(PHP_JIT_LLVM_DUMP=text only those whose name contains text). Compiled
functions are named after the PHP code they come from ("jit:Class::method@
file", "jit:TRACE-$..."), so Fil-C panics in JIT code name it. The
persistent cache applies to the C backend only.

The LLVM backend shares compiled code between processes forked after
startup, such as FPM workers, through FilLLVMJIT_EnableSharedObjectCache:
an identical module with identical mappings links the object another
worker produced instead of compiling again. For modules to be identical
across workers, values that differ between them stay out of the IR: a
pointer into opcache's shared memory (whose address depends on the
process's mapping), each exit's link target, and the trace number are
read from externally initialized globals of the module, filled in after
compiling; trace names leave out the trace number.
PHP_JIT_LLVM_CACHE_SIZE sets the region's size (e.g. 256M) and turns the
cache on; it is off by default. Fil-C code has no capability to the region,
but the runtime's refusal of writable /proc/<pid>/mem opens can be raced,
and code that escapes that way could plant code for every worker instead of
only its own process.

